This installment opens with the conclusion of Thor’s cauldron quest from `maji_kadavulgal8.md`. The English leading ellipsis marks that inherited sentence boundary. It ends immediately before Tiamat summons Kingu and declares war in `maji_kadavulgal10.md`.

The source uses adapted or OCR-variable spellings for Norse/Teutonic names: `லாக்` (Loki), `ஓடின்` (Odin), `தார்` (Thor), `ஜோட்டுன்` (Jotun), `பிரஜியா/பிஜியா/பிரிஜியா` (Freyja), `சிப்` (Sif), `ப்ரோக்` (Brok), `திரிபூம்` (Thrym), `பால்டர்` (Balder), and `மிட்கார்டு` (Midgard). Conventional English forms are used without modifying the Tamil source.

Babylonian names likewise follow source forms: `மெரோடாக்` (Marduk), `அப்சூ` (Apsu), `டியாமட்` (Tiamat), `லச்மு/லச்சாமூ` (Lahmu/Lahamu), `அன்ஷர்` (Anshar), `கிஷார்` (Kishar), `அனு` (Anu), `ஈயா` (Ea), and `மம்மூ` (Mummu). The OCR form `டாம்ீனோ` is uncertain; the translation uses the conventionally associated name Damkina and records the uncertainty here.

The source appears to conflate or compress several mythic roles and contains damaged fragments, including the opening quotation under “Babylonian Creation.” These are preserved verbatim in the Tamil block; the translation follows the evident reading without silently rewriting the source.
